# Routes and Handlers

This tutorial builds an order Agent that uses each executable route form, then shows how a Signal selects a route and how a route maps output to state and a reply.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "migration/consolidated-v3"}
])
```

## Write the Actions

`SubmitOrder` returns complete next state, which is the default contract for a route. `PriceOrder` returns a larger output with named parts, so its route must say which part to use. A route can select a field only when the output schema declares it.

```elixir
defmodule RoutesGuide.SubmitOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "routes_guide_submit_order",
    schema:
      Jido.Joi.object(%{
        sku: Jido.Joi.string(),
        channel: Jido.Joi.string()
      })

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{sku: sku, channel: channel}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | last_sku: sku, channel: channel, handled_by: "submit"}}
  end
end

defmodule RoutesGuide.PriceOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "routes_guide_price_order",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{quantity: Jido.Joi.integer()}),
    output_schema:
      Jido.Joi.object(%{
        next_state: Jido.Joi.map(),
        quote: Jido.Joi.object(%{total_cents: Jido.Joi.integer()})
      })

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{quantity: quantity}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok,
     %{
       next_state: %{state | quotes: state.quotes + 1},
       quote: %{total_cents: quantity * 250}
     }}
  end
end

defmodule RoutesGuide.AddNote do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "routes_guide_add_note",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{note: Jido.Joi.string()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{note: note}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | notes: [note | state.notes]}}
  end
end
```

## Declare the routes

Routes live in the `routes` section. The first argument is a Signal type, or a path with wildcards.

```elixir
defmodule RoutesGuide.OrderAgent do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "order"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{
             last_sku: Jido.Joi.string() |> Jido.Joi.nullable() |> Jido.Joi.default(nil),
             channel: Jido.Joi.string() |> Jido.Joi.default("none"),
             handled_by: Jido.Joi.string() |> Jido.Joi.default("none"),
             quotes: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0),
             notes: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.string()) |> Jido.Joi.default([])
           })
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/shop/order"

    # An Action module, a Signal helper, and a default for omitted input.
    route "order.submit", RoutesGuide.SubmitOrder,
      as: :submit,
      defaults: %{channel: "web"}

    # An Action module whose output needs a selected state and a reply.
    route "order.price", RoutesGuide.PriceOrder, as: :price do
      state from: output(:next_state)
      reply "order.priced", from: output(:quote)
    end

    # An inline Action. The pattern matches validated input.
    route "order.cancel", as: :cancel do
      action %{reason: reason},
        schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{reason: Jido.Joi.string()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | handled_by: "cancel:" <> reason}}
      end
    end

    # An inline Flow. Its output is the next state.
    route "order.annotate", as: :annotate do
      flow schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{note: Jido.Joi.string()}) do
        step "add_note", action: RoutesGuide.AddNote, params: %{note: input(:note)}
        output result("add_note")
      end
    end

    # A read that keeps the current state.
    route "order.inspect", RoutesGuide.PriceOrder, as: :inspect, defaults: %{quantity: 0} do
      state :unchanged
    end

    # One wildcard segment, then a fallback for everything else.
    route "order.audit.*", RoutesGuide.AddNote, defaults: %{note: "audited"}

    route "**", RoutesGuide.AddNote, defaults: %{note: "unrouted"}
  end
end

order = RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.instantiate!(id: "order-1")
order.state.quotes
```

The cell returns `0`. A route has exactly one handler. It can be an Action or Flow module, an inline `action`, an inline `flow`, a `transform`, or a `forward`. See [Transforms and Forwards](route-transforms-and-forwards.md) for the last two.

A positional module can be an Action or a Flow. Jido resolves which one it is. The route for `order.submit` would look the same with a Flow module.

## Merge defaults and input

Route defaults merge once with the Signal data, and the Signal wins. The merge is shallow.

```elixir
{:ok, %{state: from_default}, []} =
  RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.cmd(order, RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.submit_signal!(%{sku: "A-1"}))

{:ok, %{state: from_signal}, []} =
  RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.cmd(
    order,
    RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.submit_signal!(%{sku: "A-1", channel: "phone"})
  )

{from_default.channel, from_signal.channel}
```

The cell returns `{"web", "phone"}`. Only Action and Flow routes accept `:defaults`.

## Run the inline handlers

```elixir
{:ok, cancelled, []} =
  RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.cmd(order, RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.cancel_signal!(%{reason: "duplicate"}))

{:ok, annotated, []} =
  RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.cmd(order, RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.annotate_signal!(%{note: "gift wrap"}))

{cancelled.state.handled_by, annotated.state.notes}
```

The cell returns `{"cancel:duplicate", ["gift wrap"]}`.

## Select state and a reply

By default, the complete output of an Action or Flow becomes the next state. Two declarations change that:

- `state from: output(path)` selects one output field as the next state. `state :unchanged` keeps the current state and ignores the output.
- `reply type, from: output(path)` adds a `Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply` to the Turn with the selected data.

`output()` selects the whole output. `output(:field)` or `output([:field, :nested])` selects a path.

```elixir
{:ok, priced, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.cmd(order, RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.price_signal!(%{quantity: 4}))

{priced.state.quotes, reply.type, reply.data}
```

The cell returns `{1, "order.priced", %{total_cents: 1000}}`. A reply route needs a `signal_source`, because that becomes the reply's source.

```elixir
{:ok, inspected, []} =
  RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.cmd(order, RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.inspect_signal!())

inspected === order
```

The cell returns `true`, because `state :unchanged` kept the source state. [Agent Turns and Replies](turns-state-and-replies.md) explains how a caller receives a reply.

## Select one route

Selection takes the first declared route that matches. An exact path has no priority over a wildcard, so put the specific route first. `*` matches one segment, and `**` matches any number.

`Jido.Agent.Route.select/2` shows the choice without running a handler.

```elixir
definition = RoutesGuide.OrderAgent.definition()

for type <- ["order.submit", "order.audit.weekly", "invoice.created"] do
  {:ok, route} = Jido.Agent.Route.select(definition, type)
  {type, route.path}
end
```

The cell returns:

```text
[
  {"order.submit", "order.submit"},
  {"order.audit.weekly", "order.audit.*"},
  {"invoice.created", "**"}
]
```

Selection happens before the handler runs. A failing handler returns its error, and Jido does not try the next route.

Without a matching route, a Turn returns an error. This Agent has a `**` fallback, so a separate definition shows that case.

```elixir
strict =
  Jido.Agent.define!(%{
    name: "strict_order",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{notes: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.string()) |> Jido.Joi.default([])}),
    routes: [%{path: "order.note", handler: %{kind: :action, target: RoutesGuide.AddNote}}]
  })
  |> Jido.Agent.instantiate!(id: "strict-1")

other = Jido.Signal.new!("order.ship", %{}, source: "/shop")
{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{code: code}} = Jido.Agent.cmd(strict, other)
code
```

The cell returns `:unmatched_input`.

A Plugin that implements route selection can choose a different matching route. See [Plugin Routing](../plugins/plugin-input-and-routes.md).

## Declare routes as data

The same fields work in `Jido.Agent.define/1`. The `state` and `reply` selectors use `%{source: :output, path: [...]}`. Omit `state` for the default.

```elixir
{:ok, pricing} =
  Jido.Agent.define(%{
    name: "pricing",
    signal_source: "/shop/pricing",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{quotes: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)}),
    routes: [
      %{
        path: "order.price",
        handler: %{kind: :action, target: RoutesGuide.PriceOrder},
        as: :price,
        defaults: %{quantity: 1},
        state: %{source: :output, path: [:next_state]},
        reply: %{type: "order.priced", from: %{source: :output, path: [:quote]}}
      }
    ]
  })

hd(pricing.routes).state
```

The cell returns `%{path: [:next_state], source: :output}`.

## Route validation errors

Definition checks run at compile time for a module, and in `Jido.Agent.define/1` for a Map. Each one has a `code` and a `path`.

```elixir
price = %{kind: :action, target: RoutesGuide.PriceOrder}

cases = [
  # The output schema does not declare this field.
  [%{path: "order.price", handler: price, state: %{source: :output, path: [:missing]}}],
  # A path can appear once.
  [%{path: "order.price", handler: price}, %{path: "order.price", handler: price}],
  # A helper requires an exact path.
  [%{path: "order.*", handler: price, as: :price}],
  # A helper name starts with a lowercase letter.
  [%{path: "order.price", handler: price, as: :Price}],
  # A reply route needs an Agent signal_source.
  [
    %{
      path: "order.price",
      handler: price,
      reply: %{type: "order.priced", from: %{source: :output, path: [:quote]}}
    }
  ]
]

for routes <- cases do
  {:error, error} = Jido.Agent.define(%{name: "bad_order", routes: routes})
  error.code
end
```

The cell returns `[:unknown_output_field, :duplicate, :wildcard_helper, :invalid_helper_name, :reply_source_required]`.

